-- title:
--   Ordinary corner count against supersingular polar differentials
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime with $p \neq 2$ and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$; $H \leq (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ is a subgroup containing every unit whose image under `ZMod.unitsMap` for $M/p \mid M$ is $1$ (hypothesis `hHp`), $S$ is a set of natural numbers, and `hin` is the predicate [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L113), which asserts that for every prime $\ell$ the Hecke inputs `HeckeInputsHAlong` hold over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ at level $(M,H)$ and that for every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ there is an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebras of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar`. Write $J =$ `JH M H`, the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of that function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and $T =$ [`TateModule p J`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$.
--
--   Hecke data. $\mathbb{T}$ is a commutative ring which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and acts on $T$ compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action, and this action is faithful (`hfaith`: an element annihilating all of $T$ is zero). A map `op` sends each generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) — the inductive family consisting of $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and the diamonds $\langle d \rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ — to an element of $\mathbb{T}$ acting on $T$ as `tateGenOpH`, the Tate-module endomorphism induced by the covariant operator `genOpH M H S g` on $J$ (`hop`), and the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb{T}$ (`hgen`). Furthermore $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb{T}$: finitely many complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ exhausting all maximal ideals of $\mathbb{T}$ and satisfying $e_i \in \mathfrak{m}_j \iff i \neq j$; $i_0$ is an index, and the ordinarity hypothesis `hord` requires $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$.
--
--   The place and the pinned submodule. $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ in its nonunits (`hPl`), whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$. $P_0$ is a $\mathbb{T}$-submodule of $T$ characterised by `hP0`: an element lies in $P_0$ exactly when it lies in [`IharaLemma.cornerSubmodule (S'.e i₀)`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L46), the image of multiplication by $e_{i_0}$ on $T$, and is fixed up to the $p$-adic cyclotomic character by the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ acting through `JH.tateGaloisRep`.
--
--   Differentials in characteristic $p$. $K$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra, and $\tau$ is a ring homomorphism from $\mathbb{T}$ to the $K$-linear endomorphisms of [`ModularCurve.ssPolarDifferentials K (CohCarrier.GammaH (M/p) H') p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L35), where $H' =$ `infSubgroup p M H hpM` is the image of $H$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^{\times}$ and the module in question is the space of differentials of `qExpFunctionFieldC K (GammaH (M/p) H')` over $K$ which are regular outside the supersingular places `ssPlacesQExp` and have at worst simple poles there. The compatibility `hτ` states that for every generator $g$ the operator $\tau(\mathrm{op}(g))$ agrees, as a map on differentials, with [`ModularCurve.genDiffModL K p M H hpM S g`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L266), i.e. with the Hecke map `heckeDiffModLH` for $T_\ell$ and for $U_q$ with $q \neq p$, with the Frobenius pushforward `frobPushDiffModL` for $U_p$, and with `diamondDiffModLH` at the image of $d$ for $\langle d \rangle$.
--
--   Integral models, the Néron object and the Abel–Jacobi pinning. The hypothesis `hj` places the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` in the full-level function field; $\mathfrak{X}$ is a Deligne–Rapoport model datum `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` over the ring $R(p)$, $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData` at level $M/p$ over $Pl$ (a relative group law whose generic points are indexed by `JH (M/p) H'` and whose points over the residue field are indexed by $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the reduced curve), and `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation built from $\Lambda$ represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the level-$\Gamma_N$ model, cut out by the fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero condition `algEquivZeroCut`. Next $O$ is a [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53) for these data: a smooth, separated, surjective group object $O.G \to$ `base p` with $O.\mathrm{pts} : J \simeq$ sections over the generic point, Hecke endomorphisms, a toric rank, and the further clauses of that structure. The hypotheses `hD`, `hDQ` say that the designation built from $O$ represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\mathfrak{X}$ and of its base change to $\mathbb{Q}$, `hsep` that the base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ is separated, and the block consisting of $ajQ$, $kQ$, $\overline{aj}$, $\overline{\varepsilon}$ together with `hpoinc`, `hajQε`, `hajQ`, `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_law` and `hAJ` (summarised here) pins an Abel–Jacobi morphism: the Poincaré bundle pulled back along $ajQ$ is the line bundle of the point divisor tensored with the ideal module of the zero section, $\overline{aj}$ is the induced morphism on the generic geometric fibre, $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law of `hD`, and for any two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ sitting over the chosen section there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $[x] - [s]$ whose class satisfies $O.\mathrm{pts}(\text{class}) = x \circ \overline{aj}$.
--
--   The inertia ring. $R$ is a henselian local domain with algebraically closed residue field, faithfully embedded in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with image inside $Pl$ (`hRA`), a discrete valuation ring (`hRdvr`) in which $p$ is irreducible (`hRirr`), and the hypotheses `hRfix`, `hRmax` identify $R$ with the inertia-invariants: an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ precisely when it fixes $R$ pointwise, and every element of $Pl$ fixed by inertia comes from $R$.
--
--   The $p$-divisible layers. $\mathcal{G}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) — levels $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, finite free Hopf $R$-algebras of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective coalgebra transitions whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals — and $\Delta$ is an injective additive map from the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{G}$ to $J$ which is levelwise onto the finite parts, in the sense that $y \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ iff $y = \Delta$ of a level-$v$ point (`hΔlev`), is equivariant for automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ that are $R$-linear (`hΔgal`), and transports, for each generator $g$, a transition-compatible coalgebra endomorphism family onto `genOpH M H S g` (`hΔhecke`). Further, $\mathcal{B}$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$ and $hB = 2h'$, and $\psi$ is a family of coalgebra maps $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, compatible with transitions (`hψt`), such that a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ has trivial $\mathcal{B}$-image iff its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ (`hψker`), every $\mathcal{B}$-point arises in this way (`hψsurj`), a point whose $\mathcal{B}$-image reduces to the identity (all values of the point differ from the counit by elements of $Pl$-valuation $< 1$) itself reduces to the identity (`hψred`), and for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\mathcal{B}$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity (`hperiod`).
--
--   Realisation inside the Néron object. A ring map $\rho_h : R(p) \to R$ compatible with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ are given, with the block `hιbase`, `hιcl`, `hιp`, `hιpts`, `hιmul`, `hιt`, `hιhecke`, `hιfin` (summarised here) asserting: $\iota_v$ lies over $\rho_h$; the induced map to the pullback is a closed immersion; $\iota_v$ is killed by multiplication by $p^v$; $\iota_v$ computes $O.\mathrm{pts} \circ \Delta$ on points; $\iota_v$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law; the $\iota_v$ are compatible with the transitions; each Hecke generator is realised by a coalgebra endomorphism family making $\iota_v$ equivariant and inducing `genOpH`; and the comparison morphism $j_v$ from $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ into the $p^v$-kernel over $R$ is an open and closed immersion whose image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$.
--
--   The Atkin–Lehner datum. $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is an element of `SemilinearAut` of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`, i.e. a pair of a ring automorphism of the function field and one of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ intertwined by the structure map, and `hwgen` says that $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ computes on places the involution $\mathfrak{X}.w$ of the model. Moreover $\theta$ is an automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that whenever the Laurent series of $f$ coincides with that of an element $u$ of the level-$(M/p)$ field, the series of $\theta f$ is `qExpand` of that of $u$ at $p$ (`hθ`), and `hwθ` identifies $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ with the image of $\theta$ under `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut`.
--
--   Specialisation data. A ring map $\rho : R(p) \to Pl$ compatible with the embedding into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`) is given, with $\Lambda.\sigma_A = \mathrm{Spec}\,\rho$ (`hσA`). The hypothesis `hsp` (a long list of binders, summarised here) states: for $i \in \{0,1\}$, two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $y_1, y_2$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with lifts $u_1, u_2$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho$ whose images lie in the smooth locus, matching points $u_{\kappa,1}, u_{\kappa,2}$ of the special fibre, closed points $P_1, P_2$ of the special-fibre curve model lying over them through the $i$-th component, a degree-zero divisor $D_v = [y_1] - [y_2]$, and an admissible gluing datum whose $i$-th divisor component is $[P_1] - [P_2]$, whose other divisor component and whose unit component vanish, there exists a section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of the class of $D_v$ equal to $s$ restricted along the geometric point, and whose specialisation corresponds under $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ to the glued class of the gluing datum. The hypothesis `hspΛ` is the analogous statement one level down, through the two degeneracy maps $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$ and $\mathfrak{X}.\pi_w$: it produces a section $s_0$ of $\Lambda.f$ with $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,i(\text{class of } D_v))$ given by $s_0$ and specialisation the class of $D_w = [Q_1] - [Q_2]$. The hypothesis `hdia0` says that the diamond automorphism $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{dia0}\,e$ of the model induces on places of the special-fibre curve model the action of `diamondActionModL` at [`CuspForm.gammaLift (M/p) e`](def/CuspForm_HeckeOperatorFormsGammaH.html#L36). A unit $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ is given whose image in $\mathbb{Z}/(M/p)$ is $p$ (`hd`) and such that its image or the negative thereof lies in $H'$ (`hdH`), and $M/p$ is nonzero.
--
--   Frobenius, diamond and degeneracy on the special fibre. Additive endomorphisms $F$, $F^{-1}$, $F^{*}$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the reduced level-$\Gamma_N$ function field over the residue field of $Pl$ are given with $F =$ `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL` (`hF`), $F^{-1}$ a two-sided inverse of $F$ (`hFinv`), and $F^{*} = p \cdot F^{-1}$ (`hFstar`); a unit $pb$ reducing to $p$ (`hpb`) and the diamond operator $\delta$ given by `diamondActionModL` at `gammaLift (M/p) pb` (`hδ`). Finally $\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}} : \{0,1\} \to \mathrm{Hom}(\mathrm{JH}(M/p, H'), J)$ and sections $\mathrm{degPull}\,i$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.f$ satisfy: $O.\mathrm{pts}(\alpha_{\mathrm{pull}}\,i\,x) = \Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}\,i$ (`hpull`); on specialisations, `GluedPic0.toPic0Pair` of the specialisation of $\mathrm{degPull}\,i$ is $(z, F^{*}z)$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^{*}z, \delta z)$ for $i = 1$, where $z$ is the class corresponding to the given section (`hpullsp`); $\overline{W}$ is the endomorphism of $J$ given by the action of $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ (`hWbar`); the relation `hUPgen` holds: `genOpH M H S (U p)` $x + \overline{W} x = \alpha_{\mathrm{pull}}\,1(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0\,x)$ for all $x \in J$; and $\mathrm{degPull}\,i$ is compatible with the relative group laws (`hpull_mul`).
--
--   Conclusion. Write $e = S'.e\,i_0$, let $C =$ [`IharaLemma.cornerSubmodule e`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L46) $= e \cdot T$, and let $X$ be the image of the underlying additive subgroup of $C$ under [`TateModule.proj p J 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122), the evaluation of a Tate-module element at index $1$. Then
--   $$p^{\,\dim_K \operatorname{range} \tau(e)} \cdot \#\{x \in X : \exists\, y \in \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Point}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})\,1,\ \Delta(y) = x \text{ and } v_{Pl}\bigl(y(a) - a \text{'s counit image}\bigr) < 1 \text{ for all } a \in \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,1\} = \#X,$$
--   where the exponent is the $K$-dimension of the range of the endomorphism $\tau(e)$ of the supersingular polar differentials, the middle factor is the `Set.ncard` of the set of those $x$ in $X$ which are $\Delta$-images of a level-one point of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ reducing to the identity (every value of the associated $R$-algebra map differs from the image of the counit by an element of $Pl$-valuation less than $1$), and the right-hand side is `Nat.card` of $X$.
--
--   This is the counting step for the ordinary corner cut out by the idempotent $e_{i_0}$: it measures the index in $e_{i_0}J[p]$ of the part captured by the finite layer of the $p$-divisible group over the inertia ring against the $K$-dimension of the corresponding corner of the supersingular polar differentials on $X_{H'}(M/p)$ in characteristic $p$. It is obtained by combining the analogous count for the regular differentials with the count for the residue image of the corner, and it feeds the construction of the $\mathrm{dlog}$-type homomorphism from the $p$-torsion to the supersingular polar differentials in [`ModularCurve.exists_addMonoidHom_torsion_ssPolarDifferentials_dlog_of_ordinary_of_mem_infSubgroup`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_addMonoidHom_torsion_ssPolarDifferentials_dlog_of_ordinary_of_mem_infSubgroup).
